---
title: "The websocket protocol"
---

The preview is one long-lived **websocket** carrying **line-delimited JSON**: one
JSON object per text frame, no framing of its own beyond what the websocket gives
you. The server is the producer (it pushes renders and diffs); the browser is the
consumer (it applies them to the DOM). A small client-to-server back-channel
carries click-to-source reports and the "restart kernel" action, and nothing else.

The message shapes are defined **once**, in `crates/server/src/protocol.rs`, and
shared by both dev servers: the single-document [`serve.rs`](server.tmd) and the
multi-page `serve_site.rs` push the *same* messages, and `web-client/client.js` is
the other end of the contract. A contract test (`protocol_contract`) and the
client's `// @ts-check` typedefs lock the two ends to one shape, so the server and
the browser can't drift apart. This chapter is the catalog of that contract.

::: {.callout-note}
This page documents the *wire* protocol. How the diff that produces these ops is
computed (and why it is `O(n log n)`) lives in [the block model](block-model.tmd);
how the server's rebuild loop drives it lives in [the dev server](server.tmd).
:::

## Server → client messages

Every message is a JSON object with a `type` field; the client switches on it.
Nine types travel this direction.

| `type` | When it is sent | Client does |
|---|---|---|
| `full_render` | on connect, and after a change too large for ops | replace the whole body + diagnostics |
| `update` | a block's HTML changed | replace one element in place |
| `insert` | a new block appeared | build + place one element |
| `remove` | a block disappeared | drop one element |
| `set_meta` | a block's *source position* shifted but its content didn't | patch its position attributes only |
| `diagnostics` | the issue list changed (alone, or alongside block ops) | refresh the dev-menu issue stack |
| `style` | only the theme CSS changed | hot-swap the theme `<style>` in place |
| `error` | a render/read failure | show an error overlay |
| `reload` | a config / kernel-restart change | full page reload |

### `full_render`

The whole document body and the diagnostics list, in one message. Sent as the
first message on every connect, and again whenever a change is too large to express
as ops (recovering from a failed render, or a structural deck change).

```json
{
  "type": "full_render",
  "title": "Cooling coffee",
  "body_html": "<h1 ...>…</h1><p ...>…</p>…",
  "diagnostics": [
    { "level": "warning", "message": "kernel unavailable", "file": "post.tmd", "line": 12, "frame": "…" }
  ]
}
```

- `title` is the document title (or `null`); the client sets `document.title`.
- `body_html` is the full inner HTML of `#tali-root`.
- `diagnostics` is an array of issue objects (see [the shape below](#the-diagnostic-shape)).

The client handles it specially on the **first** message. Because the server
already server-rendered the body into `#tali-root` for the first paint, the first
`full_render` after connect is byte-identical, so the client **skips re-mounting
it** (no flash, no needless deck or `{js}` re-init) and only wires up diagnostics.
A *reconnect* re-mounts normally: it tears down all prior `{js}` cells first (so
their WebGL contexts and animation loops are released) and replaces the body.

### `update`

One block's HTML changed; replace exactly that element.

```json
{ "type": "update", "target_id": "b-3f2a1c0d9e8b", "html": "<pre ...>…</pre>" }
```

The client finds the element by `data-block-id` equal to `target_id`, tears down
any `{js}` cells inside the outgoing block (resolving their `invalidation`), then
`replaceWith`s the new HTML and pulses it with `.tali-flash`. This is also the op a
code cell's **output block** takes when an upstream cell re-runs: its id is keyed
to the cell, so the body differs while the id stays put.

### `insert`

A new block appeared; place it after a given block (or at the top).

```json
{ "type": "insert", "after_id": "b-3f2a1c0d9e8b", "html": "<p ...>…</p>" }
```

`after_id` is the id of the preceding block, or `null` to `prepend` at the start of
`#tali-root`. Because block ids are unique per document, the client first drops any
element already carrying the incoming id (tearing down its `{js}` cells) before
inserting. The server emits **all `remove`s before any `insert`s**, so that defense
is normally a no-op; it only matters if a reorder (which splits a moved block into a
`remove` + `insert` of the same id) ever arrived out of order.

### `remove`

A block disappeared; drop it.

```json
{ "type": "remove", "target_id": "b-3f2a1c0d9e8b" }
```

The client finds it by id, tears down any `{js}` cells inside it, and removes it.

### `set_meta`

This is the subtle one, and the common one. An edit *above* a block shifts its
line numbers without changing its content. The block's HTML is byte-identical (same
content-hash id), so re-`update`ing it would needlessly rebuild the element and drop
its live DOM state; but its source position is now stale, which would break
click-to-source. `set_meta` patches **only** the position attributes on the
existing element.

```json
{ "type": "set_meta", "target_id": "b-3f2a1c0d9e8b", "sourcepos": "42:1-44:18", "source_file": null }
```

The client sets `data-sourcepos` to the new value, and sets or removes
`data-source-file` depending on whether `source_file` is present. It runs **no**
post-change work (the content is unchanged), so a live block (a video mid-play, a
`{js}` widget, an open `<details>`) keeps its DOM state *and* still maps to the
right source line.

### `diagnostics`

The issue list changed (the kernel came back, an include resolved). A standalone
`diagnostics` message is sent on **every** rebuild where the issues differ, whether
or not the body also changed: the rebuild sends its block ops (or a `full_render`)
first, then this message follows so the dev-menu issue stack refreshes alongside any
body update.

```json
{
  "type": "diagnostics",
  "messages": [
    { "level": "warning", "message": "unresolved include: partial.tmd", "file": "post.tmd", "line": 7 }
  ]
}
```

Note the field name: `full_render` carries the array under `diagnostics`, but a
standalone update carries it under **`messages`**.

#### The diagnostic shape

Both `full_render` and `diagnostics` carry the same per-issue object:

```json
{ "level": "warning", "message": "…", "file": "post.tmd", "line": 12, "frame": "…" }
```

- `level` is `"warning"` or `"error"`.
- `message` is the human-readable text.
- `file` (optional) is the source file for click-to-source, relative to the doc's
  base dir; absent means "the document being previewed" (the client falls back to
  its own path).
- `line` (optional) is the 1-based line. When it is present the client makes the row
  **clickable** (it jumps to source).
- `frame` (optional) is a small code frame (a few source lines around `line`, with
  the offending one marked) shown inline under the row.

### `style`

Only the theme CSS changed. Rather than reload, the client hot-swaps the contents
of `<style id="qmd-theme">` in place (creating the element if absent).

```json
{ "type": "style", "css": ":root { --tali-accent: #4c8dff; … }" }
```

Because nothing in the body is rebuilt, scroll position, the current deck slide, and
open callouts all survive a theme edit. A combined content-plus-theme save sends the
block ops *and* a `style` message in the same rebuild, so both update with no reload.

### `error`

A render or read failure (a panic in the render path, a file that won't read). The
client switches its status to `error` and shows the message as an overlay.

```json
{ "type": "error", "message": "internal render error: …" }
```

The server keeps serving: the rebuild loop catches the panic, broadcasts this
error, and the **next good save recovers** with a fresh `full_render`. See [panic
isolation](server.tmd#panic-isolation).

### `reload`

A blunt instrument: tell the client to do a full `location.reload()`. Sent when a
block diff can't express the change, for example after a **kernel restart** (so
`{js}` cells re-bind to freshly-defined `define` values) or, in a site, a
`_site.yml` change that re-discovers the whole project.

```json
{ "type": "reload" }
```

## Client → server messages

The back-channel is deliberately tiny. The browser is a **read-only view**: it
never sends edits, only navigations and one control action. Two types travel this
direction.

| `type` | When | Server does |
|---|---|---|
| `click_block` | the author Alt/Option-clicks a block | logs the source location to the console |
| `restart_kernel` | the dev-menu "Restart kernel" action | flags a restart + wakes the rebuild loop |

### `click_block`

Reports the block the author Alt-clicked, so the location shows in the dev console.
(The actual jump-to-editor happens browser-side, or via the editor companion's
relay; see [click-to-source](#click-to-source-and-reverse-sync) below.)

```json
{ "type": "click_block", "block_id": "b-3f2a1c0d9e8b", "source_file": null, "sourcepos": "42:1-44:18" }
```

### `restart_kernel`

```json
{ "type": "restart_kernel" }
```

Sets a flag and kicks the rebuild loop, which drops and respawns the kernel,
re-runs every cell, and broadcasts a `reload`.

::: {.callout-warning}
## The server does not validate inbound messages

`handle_client_msg` parses the JSON, switches on `type`, and reads each field with
a fallback (a missing `source_file` logs as `(primary)`, a missing `sourcepos` as
`?`); an unknown `type` or unparseable frame is silently ignored. There is no
schema check and no per-connection auth on these messages. That is **intentional**
under the [trust model](block-model.tmd#trust-model): taliesin serves one author's
own documents, and the only state these messages reach is the author's local
render or kernel (worst case, a LAN peer triggers a kernel restart). A
multi-tenant or hosted deployment would have to add authorization here.
:::

## The connect handshake

The single most important detail of the protocol is *ordering*: a client that
connects mid-rebuild must not miss an op or apply one twice. The handshake closes
that window by taking the same lock the rebuild loop uses.

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-handshake
%%| fig-cap: "The connect handshake. The snapshot and the subscription are taken under one lock, so the client's first full_render and the op stream that follows cannot interleave with a concurrent rebuild."
sequenceDiagram
  participant C as client.js
  participant S as serve.rs (ws upgrade)
  participant L as doc lock + broadcast
  C->>S: WebSocket upgrade (Origin checked first)
  S->>L: lock doc
  L-->>S: subscribe to broadcast + snapshot full_render
  S->>L: unlock
  S-->>C: full_render (initial)
  loop on each save
    L-->>S: ops broadcast (under the lock)
    S-->>C: update / insert / remove / set_meta
  end
```

When a client upgrades, the server does two things **under one lock**: it
`subscribe`s to the broadcast channel *and* snapshots the current document as a
`full_render`. Because the rebuild loop also takes that lock to broadcast its ops,
the snapshot and the subscription happen as one atomic step: the client is
guaranteed to receive the snapshot first and then every op *after* it, with none
straddling the boundary. The client sends the snapshot, then `select!`s between
incoming control messages and outgoing broadcasts.

If a slow client ever falls behind and the broadcast channel **lags**, the server
re-syncs it with a fresh `full_render` rather than trying to replay missed ops.

## Guarding the channel

Because the control channel lives on the websocket, two orthogonal guards protect
it. Both are in `serve.rs` and apply to both servers.

### Cross-origin guard (always on)

A page on another site must not be able to open a websocket against your dev server
and drive the control channel (a CSRF on the control channel). `origin_allowed`
compares the upgrade request's `Origin` against its `Host`:

- **No `Origin`** means the request isn't from a browser (a non-browser client sends
  none), so it is allowed.
- **`Origin` authority equals `Host`** (same origin) is allowed. This also covers
  the LAN case: a phone dials the exact `Host` it sees.
- **`Origin` host is loopback** (`localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, `::1`, `[::1]`) is allowed.
- Anything else gets a **`403`** ("cross-origin websocket refused").

This is always installed, with or without `--host`, because a browser always sends
`Origin`, so it blocks cross-site driving without affecting non-browser clients.

### LAN access token (`--host` only)

By default the server binds loopback only. `--host` binds `0.0.0.0` so a phone on
the same network can open the preview, which also means anyone on the LAN could
reach it. A **per-session token** gates non-loopback access.

| Caller | Token needed? | Outcome |
|---|---|---|
| Loopback (your own machine, the [VS Code companion](extending.tmd)) | no | always allowed |
| LAN peer with valid `?t=` in the URL | yes | allowed; server sets a `qmd_token` cookie |
| LAN peer with a valid `qmd_token` cookie | yes | allowed |
| LAN peer with no / wrong token | yes | `403` |

The token is a random UUID minted at startup and threaded into the printed LAN URL
and QR code as `?t=…`. The first tokened load sets a `qmd_token` cookie
(`Path=/; SameSite=Lax; Max-Age=86400`, a 24-hour expiry), so every later
same-origin request (assets, the websocket upgrade, reloads) authenticates by cookie
with no client changes. The token is regenerated each server start, so a stale cookie
simply fails closed and the author re-scans. **Loopback peers
are detected from the connection address and exempted entirely**, so the local
workflow and the editor companion never see the token. Without `--host` there is no
token and the guard is never installed at all. Full rationale lives in [the dev
server](server.tmd#lan-exposure-the-host-token).

## Click-to-source and reverse sync

Click-to-source and editor sync ride on the two source-position attributes every
block carries (`data-sourcepos`, and `data-source-file` for included blocks). This
is a separate channel from the websocket: it is the `postMessage` protocol between
`client.js` and an embedding editor host.

- **Forward (preview → editor).** Alt-clicking (Option-clicking on Mac) a block
  reads its `data-sourcepos` and `data-source-file` and opens that source line. In a
  plain browser it navigates to a `vscode://file/<path>:<line>:<col>` deep link; in
  an editor webview it posts a `qmd-goto` message up to the host. (It also sends a
  `click_block` over the websocket so the location logs to the console.) The forward
  message is `qmd-goto { type, source_file, sourcepos }`, where `source_file` is the
  source path (`null` = the previewed doc) and `sourcepos` is the `line:col` to jump
  to. The `data-block-id` click path additionally spreads `block_id` into the message;
  the located-diagnostic path omits it.
- **Reverse (editor → preview).** The client listens for a `qmd-cursor {file, line}`
  `postMessage` and calls `highlightAtLine`, which picks the **smallest** block whose
  `data-sourcepos` range covers the line in the same file (else the nearest block
  starting before it), outlines it with `.tali-hl`, and scrolls it into view, or, in a
  deck, jumps `QmdDeck` to the containing slide.

The in-repo VS Code companion is the host that drives this; it is documented in
[extending Taliesin](extending.tmd). Because the surface is just two `postMessage`
shapes, any editor can host the preview and add cursor sync without forking the
client.

::: {.callout-tip}
The websocket and the `postMessage` channel never overlap: edits never cross either
one (the preview is a read-only view), the websocket carries renders and diffs, and
`postMessage` carries cursor navigations. That clean split is what lets the
[block model](block-model.tmd) stay the single source of truth for both directions
of source mapping.
:::

The browser end of this contract, how `client.js` mounts a `full_render` and then
applies each `update` / `insert` / `remove` / `set_meta` in place, is documented in
[the browser client](client.tmd).
